#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065351 :tutorial/content "What logarithmic equation is equivalent to the equation $\\sqrt{4} = 2 ?$\n\nNotice that the given equation can be written using exponents as\n\n$$\n4^{1/2} = 2.\n$$\n\nThe logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n$a = b^x$ is equivalent to $\\log_{b}{a} = x$\n\nIf we compare $4^{1/2} = 2$ with $a = b^x,$ we get $a = 2, b = 4,$ and $x=\\dfrac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the equation $\\sqrt{4} = 2$ is equivalent to $\\log_{4}{2}= \\dfrac{1}{2}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "689eb94a-47df-5cd0-9019-d100d1621193" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2780 :tutorial/title "Converting Equations With Radicals Into Logarithmic Form"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065354 :tutorial/content "A **logarithm** (or log) is an operation that's the *inverse* of exponentiation. \n\nFor example, we know that ${\\color{blue}{2}}^{\\color{red}{3}} = 8.$ But we can also write this using a logarithm as\n\n$$\n\\log_{\\color{blue}{2}}(8) = {\\color{red}{3}}.\n$$\n\nIn words, we would say \"log of $8$ with base ${\\color{blue}{2}}$ equals ${\\color{red}{3}}$\". The **base** of the logarithm is always the base of the exponential expression.\n\nSince exponents and logarithms are opposite operations, an exponential equation can always be expressed in logarithmic form, and vice versa.\n\nIn general, the logarithm of $a$ with positive base $b \\neq 1$ is defined as follows:\n\n$a = {\\color{blue}b}^{\\color{red}x}$ is equivalent to $\\log_{\\color{blue}b}{a} = {\\color{red}x}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4581c0d9-ad31-511c-a9d0-00d5ae0d5e38" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2863 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
